{
  "module": "The transformer block",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "layernorm",
    "title": "LayerNorm and RMSNorm",
    "summary": "How a token's vector is rescaled to a standard size: LayerNorm subtracts the mean and divides by the spread of that one vector, and RMSNorm keeps only the rescaling.",
    "covers": ["layernorm", "rmsnorm"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "layers-hidden-states",
        "reason": "Normalisation rescales a token's hidden-state vector."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "what-for",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The hidden-state vectors are worked on again and again by many layers. A "
            },
            {
              "text": "normalisation",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " step rescales each token's vector to a standard size, so that what comes next receives numbers of a predictable scale."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The standard one is "
            },
            {
              "text": "LayerNorm",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 56)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-operation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "For one token's vector x, of length d_model, LayerNorm computes:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{LN}(x) = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\varepsilon}} \\cdot \\gamma + \\beta",
          "spoken": "LN of x equals x minus mu, divided by the square root of sigma squared plus epsilon, times gamma, plus beta"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Here "
            },
            {
              "text": "μ",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is the mean and "
            },
            {
              "text": "σ²",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " the variance of that vector's d_model entries, and ε is a tiny constant that avoids dividing by zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The mean and variance are taken over the "
            },
            {
              "text": "d_model features of that one token",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". Nothing else is involved."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 56)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "toy-ln",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy case with d_model = 4 and x = (1, 2, 3, 6). Ignore ε, and take γ = 1 and β = 0."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "mean μ = 3"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "variance σ² = 3.5"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "LN(x) = (-1.069, -0.535, 0.000, 1.604)"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Check: the outputs add up to 0 (their mean is 0) and their variance is exactly 1. LayerNorm always gives a vector with mean 0 and variance 1, before γ and β."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 56)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "gain-bias",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "If every output always had mean 0 and variance 1 the layer would lose freedom, so LayerNorm ends with a learned "
            },
            {
              "text": "gain γ",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " and "
            },
            {
              "text": "bias β",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", one number for each feature. They are parameters, learned in training."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "With γ = 2 and β = 1 in the toy case, the output becomes (-1.138, -0.069, 1.000, 4.207)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 56)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "no-batch",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "LayerNorm uses "
            },
            {
              "text": "no batch statistics",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": everything comes from the one token's own features. So training and inference behave identically, and it works for any batch size or sequence length."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 56)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "rmsnorm",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "RMSNorm",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is a simpler cousin. It drops the mean subtraction (the re-centring) and keeps only the rescaling:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(x) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{mean}(x^2) + \\varepsilon}} \\cdot \\gamma",
          "spoken": "RMSNorm of x equals x divided by the square root of the mean of x squared plus epsilon, times gamma"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In the toy case, mean(x²) = 12.5, so its root is about 3.536 and RMSNorm(x) = (0.283, 0.566, 0.849, 1.697). The mean of the result is 0.849, not 0, since nothing was subtracted."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The idea is that re-centring is not needed (Zhang and Sennrich, 2019). It is cheaper and performed comparably in their experiments. It is used in the Llama family, whose norm layers have only the gain γ and no bias."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 57)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
